{
  "title": "Click-Dog: Health",
  "description": "OTLP self-monitoring dashboard for click-dog. Prioritizes current action state: last success age, circuit breaker severity, error rate, export throughput, and adaptive backoff before lifetime counters. In the default one-sidecar-per-ClickHouse-node deployment the top cockpit tiles aggregate worst-case across the active exporters (max breaker state, max backoff, oldest active last-success) so a single unhealthy active exporter is never hidden by healthy peers; HA standbys stay visible through the leader state without making stale-export tiles red. Circuit breaker state uses severity order: 0=closed, 1=half-open, 2=open. Metrics are pushed over OTLP when metrics.otlp.enabled is true and grouped by host.name; the note widget keeps the legacy OpenMetrics scrape mapping for Prometheus-only users.",
  "layout_type": "ordered",
  "template_variables": [
    {
      "name": "host",
      "prefix": "host.name",
      "default": "*"
    }
  ],
  "widgets": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "definition": {
        "type": "note",
        "content": "# Click-Dog — Health\n\nOTLP self-monitoring for the click-dog exporter. The top cockpit answers whether active exports are flowing now: last success age, breaker state, current error rate, export throughput, and adaptive backoff. In the default one-sidecar-per-ClickHouse-node deployment these tiles aggregate worst-case across active exporters — `max` breaker state, `max` backoff, and oldest (`min`) `role:active` last-success timestamp — so one unhealthy active exporter is never averaged away by healthy peers while HA standbys stay visibly idle. The **Per-host health** table below the cockpit ranks sidecars worst-first so the degraded host is immediately identifiable; standbys remain visible through the leader column but are excluded from stale-export age calculations. Lifetime totals live lower on the dashboard. Metrics are pushed over OTLP when `metrics.otlp.enabled: true`; by default they reuse `exporters.otel[0]`. Set `metrics.otlp.host` for a stable `host.name` in containers. Circuit breaker state uses severity order: `0=closed`, `1=half-open`, `2=open`.\n\n**Legacy Datadog Agent OpenMetrics check (Prometheus-only)** — drop this in `/etc/datadog-agent/conf.d/openmetrics.d/conf.yaml` and restart the Agent. The explicit rename list maps Prometheus names to the legacy dotted Datadog names. The shipped dashboard queries OTLP names by default; use this block only if you are staying on the scrape path.\n\n```yaml\ninit_config:\n\ninstances:\n  - openmetrics_endpoint: http://localhost:9090/metrics\n    namespace: click_dog\n    metrics:\n      # Counters — Datadog appends `.count` and submits as a monotonic rate.\n      - click_dog_spans_exported_total: spans_exported\n      - click_dog_spans_filtered_total: spans_filtered\n      - click_dog_spans_duplicates_total: spans_duplicates\n      - click_dog_export_attempts_total: export.attempts\n      - click_dog_export_accepted_total: export.accepted\n      - click_dog_export_errors_total: export.errors\n      - click_dog_cycle_results_total: cycle_results\n      # Gauges — submitted as-is under the namespace.\n      - click_dog_circuit_breaker_state: circuit_breaker.state\n      - click_dog_leader: leader\n      - click_dog_backoff_interval_seconds: backoff_interval.seconds\n      - click_dog_last_success_timestamp_seconds: last_success_timestamp.seconds\n      - click_dog_uptime_seconds: uptime.seconds\n      - click_dog_last_cycle_duration_seconds: last_cycle.duration_seconds\n      - click_dog_last_cycle_exported_spans: last_cycle.exported_spans\n      - click_dog_last_cycle_filtered_spans: last_cycle.filtered_spans\n      - click_dog_last_cycle_duplicate_spans: last_cycle.duplicate_spans\n      # ClickHouse data-plane health (#183) — distinguishes 'click-dog is down' from 'ClickHouse is not emitting useful data'.\n      - click_dog_span_log_last_poll_timestamp_seconds: span_log.last_poll_timestamp.seconds\n      - click_dog_span_log_newest_row_age_seconds: span_log.newest_row_age.seconds\n      - click_dog_span_log_rows_last_cycle: span_log.rows_last_cycle\n      - click_dog_query_log_enrichment_attempts_total: query_log.enrichment.attempts\n      - click_dog_query_log_enrichment_successes_total: query_log.enrichment.successes\n      - click_dog_query_log_enrichment_failures_total: query_log.enrichment.failures\n      - click_dog_query_log_enrichment_match_ratio: query_log.enrichment.match_ratio\n      - click_dog_spans_with_query_id_ratio: spans_with_query_id_ratio\n      - click_dog_normalized_query_supported: normalized_query_supported\n      # Topology self-audit — detects the sidecar + use_cluster_queries anti-pattern. Grey / no-data ≠ healthy: the metric must be scraped.\n      - click_dog_topology_warning: topology_warning\n```\n\nThe OTLP path emits `click_dog.{canonical_key}` and Datadog stores monotonic sums with a `.count` suffix, for example `click_dog.spans_exported.count`. The scrape mapping above is retained for pure-Prometheus users who cannot enable OTLP self-metrics.\n\n**No data?** In Datadog Metrics Explorer, search for `click_dog.`. Confirm `metrics.otlp.enabled: true`, that the Datadog Agent or collector OTLP gRPC receiver is reachable, and that `metrics.otlp.host` matches the `host.name` template variable you expect. On the legacy scrape path, search for `click_dog.click_dog_spans_exported_total`; that means the explicit OpenMetrics `metrics:` rename list above is not being applied.",
        "background_color": "blue",
        "font_size": "14",
        "text_align": "left",
        "vertical_align": "top",
        "show_tick": false,
        "has_padding": true
      },
      "layout": {"x": 0, "y": 0, "width": 12, "height": 2}
    },
